#---
# purpose: "a legal recursive @type declaration that the compiler cannot yet resolve"
# expect: diagnostic
#---
#
# `t` is a legal recursive declaration: it is `:start` or a pair whose second
# field is another `t`. Resolving it needs a recursive denotation — a type whose
# definition names itself. The compiler has none, so `DeriveTypeDef(t)` waits on
# `TypeDefined(t)`, which is its own output. Every job that needs the spec waits
# behind it, the drive finds no ready producer, and the program is rejected on
# all three doors with that unresolved wait printed. Nothing runs.
#
# `expect: diagnostic` is satisfied here by a stall, not by a diagnostic: the
# drive emits no `fz.diag.*` event, it prints the wait it could not satisfy and
# exits nonzero. That is why this fixture pins stderr rather than a
# `diagnostic.code`, and why its golden is the smallest substring that still
# names the cycle. The harness cannot yet tell the two apart.
def main() do
  dbg(build(3))
end

@type t :: :start | {integer, t}

@spec build(integer) :: t
def build(0), do: :start
def build(n), do: {n, build(n - 1)}
